Today the runner drains: it keeps invoking the agent command until the board stops moving. For coordinated multi-repo work that is the wrong granularity — the coordinator never gets control back between tickets.

Add `mode: ticket` next to the existing drain behaviour. In ticket mode foundation picks one task, runs the agent once, classifies the result and returns. The caller decides what happens next.

The point is control, not size. A single-ticket run ends by itself, so nothing has to stop it, and reordering, agent switching and gating all become possible between runs. An agent that dies mid-work loses one attempt: its claim expires, `karr unlock` clears the pick lock, the ticket is free again.

Design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`
mode: ticket implemented, commit eaa27b8 (local, not pushed).

Decisions: (1) the id reaches the agent as $KARR_TASK plus a closing sentence spliced into $PROMPT - nothing appended to the command, so #188's kind: contract stays free; (2) foundation names the card, the agent claims it (the repo lock plus one-agent-per-repository already excludes anybody else, and #176 means the claim name must be the agent's own) - a dead agent loses its own claim to claim_timeout/karr unlock plus one attempt; (3) mode: drain|single|ticket replaces drain: true|false, which stays honoured as the older spelling (mode asked first, per-repo drain still beats a config-wide mode, unknown value errors and skips the repo).

New: App::karr::Foundation::Picker (pick eligibility + pick ranking, no claim, no lock; composes Role::ClaimTimeout so an expired claim is visible - otherwise a crashed agent's claim would silence a board for good). A ticket run is judged by its own card: progress if it moved, new outcome 'stall' if not, attempt bumped and auto-blocked at max_attempts under #158's unchanged ownership guard. Nothing assignable = no agent run at all.

